so today's topic is going to be  radiometric dating so let's go ahead and  get started so when we look at the  layers of rock beneath the surface we  see that you know any fossil and this  layer of rock is going to are going to  be the youngest and then any fossils in  the next layer are going to be a little  older any fossils in the next layer down  are gonna be a little older and any  fossils in the next layer down are gonna  be the oldest well this is what's called  relative dating relative to one another  we can figure out which of these samples  is the oldest than the youngest but  radiometric dating is a lot more precise  it can obtain the absolute age of a  fossil how many thousands or millions of  years old a sample might be  so to understand radiometric dating we  have to understand what isotopes are you  know carbon-12 and carbon-14 are two  very common examples of isotopes  atoms of the same element they're both  carbon but they contain a different  number of neutrons so when I look at the  carbon on the square on the top I see it  has the atomic number of six  well that means six protons anything  with six protons is going to be carbon  but the 12 is the mass and the mass is  when you add up the protons and neutrons  so six protons plus what mystery number  adds up to 12 well that's just six but  look at the square beneath it well it  has the atomic number of six again  that means six protons after all this is  still carbon and then the 14 means  protons and neutrons add up to 14 so six  plus what mystery number is 14 well  that's the number eight and so you see  the definition of isotopes they're both  the same element these are both carbon  but they have a different number of  neutrons now the reason this is  important is because carbon-14 is  radioactive and it will decay over a  period of time and we can measure the  decay to figure out how old a sample  might be  so let's focus on carbon-12 and  carbon-14 they're isotopes of carbon and  they're in the atmosphere in the form of  carbon dioxide so when plants take in  and other autotrophs when plants and  autotrophs take in the carbon to do  photosynthesis and they use the carbon  and they make glucose so when they take  in the carbon from the atmosphere  they're taken in not just carbon-12 but  also carbon-14 and that carbon whether  it's carbon-12 or carbon-14 is  transferred up the food chain when one  organism eats another it needs another  it eats another so when an organism is  alive you would expect the ratio of  carbon-12 and carbon-14 to be very  similar to that of the atmosphere after  all this carbon came from the atmosphere  but when an organism dies  start the clock the decay of carbon-14  begins  and over time carbon-14 were decay and  change and break down into nitrogen so  if something is recently dead the  remains will still possess a whole lot  of carbon-14 because not much time has  passed for it to decay but if a sample  is long dead then its remains will  possess much less carbon-14 because more  time has passed for it to decay well  let's talk about this decay  well let's talk about the time it takes  for an isotope to decay what's known as  its half-life and the half-life is the  time it takes for half or 50% of the  amount of isotope to decay and in the  example of carbon-14 it's been measured  to take about five thousand seven  hundred and thirty years for half of the  amount of carbon-14 in a sample to decay  well right now our cow is a living  organism and so it's going to have a  hundred percent of the carbon-14 that it  will ever have as it takes in a carbon  from you know eating plants as we just  mentioned on the previous slide but look  at the first layer buried underneath the  ground and this is a fossil of a horse  let's pretend that this fossil was  discovered and it has 50% of the  carbon-14 as those living today well we  would say that one half life has passed  which we know is 5730 years and so we  would say that this horse said this  fossil of the horse is 5730 years old  but look at the next fossil beneath it  let's pretend this fossil is found and  is discovered to have 25% of the  carbon-14 remaining well 25 is half of  50 and 50 is half of 100 so two  half-lives have passed since this  organism was alive so two times the  half-life of 5730 this fossil would  estimate to be about eleven thousand  four hundred and sixty years old look at  the next fossil beneath it let's pretend  this third fossil is found and  discovered and it is analyzed and it has  12 and a half percent of the carbon-14  as those alive today  well twelve and a half percent is half  of twenty-five twenty-five percent is  half of 50 and 50 percent is a half of  100 so in other words three half-lives  have passed since this thing was alive  and so we would estimate this to be  17,000 190 years old  the fourth fossil let's just repeat the  pattern let's pretend it has six point  two five percent of the carbon-14 as  those  today well that's half of twelve and a  half percent twelve and a half is half  of twenty five percent 25 percent is  half of fifty percent fifty percent is  half of a hundred percent in other words  four half-lives have passed since this  organism was living and so we would say  it's about twenty-two thousand nine  hundred and twenty years old and finally  at the bottom let's pretend this fish  fossil is analyzed and it has three  point one two five percent of the  carbon-14 well we're now up to five  half-lives of time have passed since  this was alive and so five times five  thousand seven hundred and thirty we  would estimate to be about twenty eight  thousand six hundred and fifty years old  well let's go ahead and plot this  information in a graph and what you see  in the graph is exactly what we just  talked about you can see at 50 percent  if a sample has 50% of the carbon-14  remaining it would estimate to be about  5 thousand seven hundred and thirty  years old if a sample has 25% of its  carbon-14 remaining it would estimate to  be eleven thousand four hundred and  sixty years old if a sample has 12 and a  half percent of its carbon-14 it would  estimate to be 17 thousand one hundred  and ninety years old well now this is  great for you know the the data that we  just talked about but what if there's  something that is discovered that is in  between well let's look at that what if  a fossil was found to have 70% of its  carbon-14 remaining well this is where  you can see that the beauty of a line  graph we can just kind of estimate by  drawn an imaginary line down and we  would say oh if it has 70% of its  carbon-14 remaining it would be roughly  around 3000 years old you know give or  take a few years of course what if a  sample what the fossil was found to have  20% of its carbon-14 remaining well then  if we draw an imaginary line down we  would say oh that's about 14,000 years  old so you can see with the decay rate  of carbon-14 we can roughly estimate  with pretty good accuracy the  ages of fossils you know as useful as  carbon dating might be you can see that  this graph does present a problem  eventually the amount of carbon-14  becomes so small that it's really  difficult to measure and so carbon  dating is only accurate to maybe about  fifty sixty thousand years if something  is older than that then other isotopes  have to be examined and one of those  other isotopes is potassium-40  it has a much longer half-life it's  half-life is 1.25 billion years when you  look at the graph the graph the line  looks similar as the carbon graph but  look at the time line on the bottom or  in billions of years so if a sample is  discovered to have 50% of its  potassium-40 remaining then we would  have say said that one half-life would  have passed and the sample would be  estimated to be about 1.25 billion years  old if a sample is found to have 25% of  its potassium-40 remaining then two  half-lives will have passed and the  sample will be will be estimated at two  and a half billion years old if a sample  has 12.5% of its potassium-40 remaining  then three half-lives will have passed  and the sample will have will be  estimated to be three point seven five  billion years old and if a sample has  6.25 percent of its potassium-40  remaining well then four half-lives will  have passed and the sample would  estimate to be about 5 billion years old  so as I wrap up this video it's because  of radiometric dating that we can say  the earth is four-and-a-half billion  years old and that humans have been  around for about 200,000 years because  by studying the decay of isotopes left  behind in fossils and rocks we can  accurately estimate the age of these  particular samples so I hope you found  this video helpful and thanks for  watching
 

